 happened  yesterday well i think given the taliban  takeover given the nervousness the fear  on the ground you've seen a lot of  people effectively heading for the door  um and we've got to make sure that we  can stabilize the situation and i think  ultimately british people would expect  us and i'm sure the american public the  same to prioritize our nationals uh  including dual nationals in afghanistan  uh but also those who have so loyally  served us that's what we're doing okay  um a large number of thugs is what i  read in this morning's newspapers that  are on their way to kabul uh this uneasy  piece that is in place at the moment is  very soon going to be shattered what's  your intelligence on that well we don't  talk about intelligence as you know you  must be concerned about it though well  there are reports all the time the  situation is very fluid the taliban have  exerted control far more rapidly than we  expect there will be  dissenting rebel  voices and actions uh what we want to  see at the moment is two things one the  evacuation conducted as stably as  possible we've had assurances at the  higher level from the taliban  through  publicly through the taliban political  commission doha in three or third  parties i spoke to the qatari foreign  minister and the pakistani foreign  minister um they've made all sorts of  commitments in the doha agreement which  dates back to 2020 we expect to hold  into account to that  and secondly then there is the  medium-term strategy which must mean  making sure that afghanistan can't be  used by terrorist groups to attack  neighbours or the west again  trying to provide the humanitarian  relief that the people in afghanistan  need given the hugely unstable situation  you've described  that's going to be very challenging we  won't obviously be giving aid directly  to the taliban we do want to provide  humanitarian relief  to the civilian population and i think  there's a much broader question about  stabilizing that 