 they're clearly in the thousands because  we've got 3 300 out already we want to  get as many of them as we physically can  out but of course it depends on the  stable stability of the situation on the  ground which is why we come back to the  point that you mentioned earlier rightly  the stability at the airport is  absolutely key and i've been working  very closely with ben wallace and the  home secretary to make sure we can do  everything we can give ourselves the  capacity every day to get as many out as  as quickly as we can given the uh the  window of opportunity for that do you  agree with your defense secretary that  sadly some people will be left behind i  think there's always a risk of that what  i'm focused on is just doing everything  physically possible to get british  nationals out to get as many of those  have loyally served us out  and also to think about how we then move  forward with an international strategy  which can stabilize the situation for  the people of afghanistan under what  time scale are you hoping to be able to  do that i think it's difficult to say  because the situation is so fluid we  were all caught by a surprise by the  speed and pace and scale of the taliban  takeover the americans have talked about  the end of august but ultimately we need  to get people out as quickly as possible  as soon as possible and it will depend  on the security situation on the ground  but come back to the point i do think  and having spoken to tony blinken my  opposite number yesterday do think that  the the airport is is more stable today  than it was yesterday and we need to  make sure that we consolidate that uh in  the days ahead okay um given that you  said that not just you but many were  taken by surprise not least the  americans as well how disappointed are  you that our military top brass um  didn't know that this was coming  well it's not just a question by the way  of the military we have a an assessment  process uh we've looked at it for 