e secured gains in that 20 years  clearly they're now at risk what we want  to do is everything we can through the  full range of diplomatic economic  sanction measures to make sure we can  use as much leverage as we conceivably  have and i'm realistic about that to try  and moderate and exercise some form of  positive influence around the regime  ahead and of course there is still and  it may be a low low chance of this still  these talks that are going on through  the taliban's political commission which  is based in doha to try and make sure  that there's a transitional government  which isn't just purely taliban now i  think we need to be released can't trust  the taliban surely you can't but at  least as long as there's a process that  they're engaged in i think we need to  test it they're a ragtag bunch of thugs  look at they uh i'm not going to dissent  from that view but they are now in power  and we now need to deal with that  reality and as all i'm just saying is we  don't have to be optimistic about it as  long as there is an engagement by that  process we want to test uh whether there  is scope to moderate the kind of regime  that we will now see in place do we rule  out returning to afghanistan in some  numbers at some stage in the future  okay i can't uh take a crystal ball out  and thanks  saying are you going to rule that out we  we we look we we seems what biden's done  we're clearly withdrawing we said when  we went into  afghanistan nato said we'd go in  together we adapted together and we'd  leave together  so we now need to deal with that reality  how sorry are you that you didn't return  from your holiday earlier okay as i said  everyone was caught  off guard by the pace scale of the  taliban takeover um the reality is as a  foreign secretary all foreign  secretaries are in this position when  we're abroad whether it's for travel  for work or for holiday we are there uh  able to respond to events so i was  engaged in cobra talking to foreign  counterparts directly  speaking to the head of our team here in  london i was doing that on an hour by  hour basis and of course i left  as soon as the situation deteriorated  and demanded it  understood but you um and the prime  minister i mean you're the de facto uh  deputy prime minister the prime minister  both of you on holiday at the same time  while afghanistan was going to hell in a  handcart  was that a dereliction of duty no i  don't think that's uh that's the right  description look the reality is would  you describe it well look we we've been  monitoring the situation in afghanistan  uh since the 2020 doha agreement very  carefully it's been very fluid from my  point of view at any num  point in time i will have eight to ten  simmering issues that can bubble up uh  and we always know not like this yes yes  yes yes i'm afraid okay may not always  be in the media eye but actually  situations where we have to give a very  close eye i always have to be one of the  biggest airlifts since the second world  war well and but we didn't predict uh  that we would be doing this on this  scale because of the taliban takeover  but look in retrospect of course i  wouldn't have gone on holiday if i'd  known that being the case equally after  um 18 months and two years of a very uh  grueling demanding schedule i think it's  right the people in those positions try  and take some leave but we're always  ready i'm always ready uh to come back  and of course the key point that i was  making is even when i was away frankly  uh wouldn't have gone away if i'd have  known that i was constantly uh handling  and managing meetings uh talking to  foreign counterparts and uh because of  technology of course able to engage in  every one of the cobra meetings i was  speaking to a former soldier yesterday  who lost both of his legs um in  afghanistan he said he picked up bits of  his mate on the road in afghanistan he  says that it was all for nothing  my heart goes out to anyone that has  served their country and suffered those  kind of losses and seen what he would  have seen um he has served his country  loyally and i think you should be proud  of what he's achieved as i mentioned  before we're there 20 years  we have  in that time not seen a terrorist group  attacked the west from afghanistan we  have seen but what's going to happen  hold on  let's but he about it wasn't all for  nothing 10 million uh girl uh children  in education many of them girls uh eight  million landmines clear we did that with  our partners just so you know for  mothers the  the maternal fatality rate so the number  of mothers that lost their life in  childbirth or in pregnancy down by  almost a half but what no so we achieved  those things but now the question is  with the taliban in place and what are  the levers we've got to moderate what  comes next and to make sure that the  gains aren't lost what do you think so  first my  my answer to him would be is his service  was not in vain but we've got a you know  hell of a challenge now we will use all  the levers that we've got whether it's  economic tools sanctions the diplomatic  leverage i think what's going to be  important in the terms of the diplomacy  is working with all the countries in the  region as well as the traditional  partners india mata  china and russia will matter difficult  interlocutors for us pakistan will  matter all of those have got to with us  work to provide some stability and we've  started that in the security council we  use our g7 presidency we'll use the un  general assembly coming up in september  i think we need a contact group  of those regional and key partners and i  think we need to see  some international leadership and we'll  be working very closely with the un and  those partners to achieve that

